WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Director Gerard Johnstone employed the "less is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.600
more" adage while reshooting his&nbsp;
eagerly anticipated robot-horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.380
flick M3GAN. What he ended up with was&nbsp;
even more disturbing than the original.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.920
Is it too early to proclaim the&nbsp;
movie of the year? In any case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.180
M3GAN has already accrued a ton of buzz&nbsp;
on the strength of its trailer alone. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.900
artificially intelligent title character&nbsp;
— with her blank stare and TikTok-ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.620
dance moves – is the clear frontrunner&nbsp;
for horror's cultiest new villain.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:31.020
M3GAN stars Allison Williams in her first&nbsp;
Blumhouse feature since 2017's Get Out. Williams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:36.780
plays Gemma, a robotics engineering whiz who takes&nbsp;
in her orphaned niece Cady, when her sister and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.700
brother-in-law tragically die in a car accident.&nbsp;
Gemma provides a companion for her traumatized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.560
niece in the form of M3GAN, or Model 3 Generative&nbsp;
Android. When the childlike droid becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.501
overprotective of Cady, she becomes infinitely&nbsp;
more violent than Gemma ever anticipated.

00:00:53.503 --> 00:00:55.920
"Are you listening to me, Brandon?"

00:00:56.341 --> 00:01:01.560
While some fans are worried that the film's PG-13&nbsp;
rating will tamp down the scares, director Gerard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.520
Johnstone insists that the teen-friendly&nbsp;
rating made M3GAN even more disturbing.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.580
In retooling M3GAN to achieve a PG-13 rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.560
Johnstone leaned into the idea that the power&nbsp;
of suggestion can be terrifying. For Johnstone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.660
reshooting certain scenes and emphasizing cutaways&nbsp;
and off-camera violence was even more disturbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.280
than straightforward gore. Johnstone said in&nbsp;
an interview with Total Film and GamesRadar+:

00:01:33.604 --> 00:01:37.680
Johnstone recalled reshooting a specific&nbsp;
scene involving the scary dog next door.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.020
The end of the scene occurs off-camera, leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.980
the dog's fate entirely to the viewer's&nbsp;
imagination. Johnstone told the outlet:

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:57.960
Other off-camera scares are surely&nbsp;
in store for those who accidentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.460
cross M3GAN's path. Giving M3GAN a PG-13&nbsp;
rating may not have been the original plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.240
but it didn't take much to walk the movie back&nbsp;
from an R rating. Johnstone said in the interview:

00:02:13.121 --> 00:02:15.000
It makes sense to court teen audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.080
given the ages and sensibilities of the&nbsp;
main characters. Johnstone continued,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.260
Furthermore, PG-13 horror films&nbsp;
aren't without precedent, and one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:30.413
in particular, was a lodestar for Johnstone.&nbsp;
The director told Total Film and GamesRadar+:

00:02:37.334 --> 00:02:41.280
Indeed, Sam Raimi's supernatural horror&nbsp;
film has a devoted cult following,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.540
despite the lower rating. Roger Ebert&nbsp;
even called Drag Me to Hell "macabre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.400
over-the-top, fun, funny, silly and loud&nbsp;
and bombastic" — characteristics it achieved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.700
without an excess of gore or adult-only&nbsp;
themes. Other beloved horror movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.780
including A Quiet Place, The Ring, and The&nbsp;
Sixth Sense are all rated PG-13 as well.

00:02:57.877 --> 00:02:59.812
"I see dead people."

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:04.440
Jason Blum also has no qualms about the PG-13&nbsp;
stamp. The producer told The Hollywood Reporter:

